 "result": "**Mechanism:** Werkzeug `LocalProxy` objects backed by a `ContextVar`. All of them are defined in `src/tessel/globals.py`.\n\n- **Shared store:** `_app_var` (`src/tessel/globals.py:40`) is a `ContextVar[AppContext]` named `\"tessel.app_ctx\"`. It holds the current `AppContext`.\n- **`active_app`** (line 44) is `LocalProxy(_app_var, \"app\")`. On each access it reads the current `AppContext` and takes its `.app` attribute, which is the `Tessel` instance.\n- **`g`** (line 47) is `LocalProxy(_app_var, \"g\")`. It resolves to the `AppContext`'s `.g` attribute, an `_AppCtxGlobals` object (from `src/tessel/ctx.py`).\n- **`request`** (line 57) is `LocalProxy(_app_var, \"request\")`. It resolves to the `AppContext`'s `.request` attribute, a `Request` (from `src/tessel/wrappers.py`).\n- **Related proxies:** `app_ctx` proxies the `AppContext` itself, and `session` follows the same pattern.\n\nEvery attribute access on a proxy looks up `_app_var` for the current context. Because it's a `ContextVar`, each thread or async task gets its own value.\n\nIf nothing is bound, the proxy raises `RuntimeError` with `_no_app_msg`. `request` and `session` use `_no_req_msg` instead.\n\nI only read `globals.py`. I didn't open `ctx.py`, so I haven't confirmed where `_app_var` gets set and reset (presumably `AppContext.push()` and `pop()`).",
